<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-augmented-matrix" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Augmented Matrix and Echelon Forms</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      When we apply elementary operations, the variable names never change <mdash /> only
      the coefficients and constants do. So we may as well drop the variables and record
      just the numbers. This bookkeeping device is the augmented matrix.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-augmented-matrix">
    <title>The Augmented Matrix</title>

    <example xml:id="eg-augmented-matrix">
      <title>Forming the Augmented Matrix</title>
      <p>
        The system of linear equations
        <md>
          <mrow>
            x_1 - 2x_2 - 7x_3 \amp = -1
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            -x_1 + 3x_2 + 6x_3 \amp = 0
          </mrow>
        </md>
        is represented by the <term>augmented matrix</term>
        <md>
          \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
            1 \amp -2 \amp -7 \amp -1 \\
            -1 \amp 3 \amp 6 \amp 0
          \end{array} \right].
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Two other matrices associated with a system of linear equations are the
        <term>coefficient matrix</term> and the <term>constant matrix</term>:
        <md>
          \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -2 \amp -7 \\ -1 \amp 3 \amp 6 \end{bmatrix},
          \qquad
          \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>

    <p>
      The three elementary operations of
      <xref ref="def-elementary-operations" text="type-global" /> become
      <term>elementary row operations</term> on the augmented matrix. We use the
      following notation, where <m>r_i</m> denotes row <m>i</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow>
          r_i \leftrightarrow r_j \amp \qquad \text{interchange rows } i \text{ and } j,
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          k \, r_i \amp \qquad \text{multiply row } i \text{ by } k \neq 0,
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          k \, r_j + r_i \amp \qquad \text{add } k \text{ times row } j \text{ to row } i.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      In a replacement <m>k\,r_j + r_i</m>, the row written <em>last</em>, <m>r_i</m>, is
      the row that changes; row <m>j</m> is only read, and is left as it was. Thus
      <m>-2r_1 + r_3</m> means <q>replace row <m>3</m> by row <m>3</m> minus twice row
      <m>1</m>,</q> and row <m>1</m> stays put. (Some books write this operation as
      <m>r_3 \to r_3 - 2r_1</m>; the meaning is the same.)
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-row-equivalent">
      <title>Row Equivalence</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Two matrices are <term>row equivalent</term> if one can be obtained from the
          other by a sequence of elementary row operations.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Since row operations on an augmented matrix are exactly elementary operations on
      the corresponding system, <xref ref="rem-operations-reversible" text="type-global"/>
      says: <em>if the augmented matrices of two systems are row equivalent, then the
      systems are equivalent</em>. This is the principle behind everything that follows.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="eg-row-operations">
      <title>Row Operations on an Augmented Matrix</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the system of linear equations
          <md>
            <mrow>
              3x_1 - 2x_2 - 7x_3 \amp = -1
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              -x_1 + 3x_2 + 6x_3 \amp = 1
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              2x_1 \phantom{{}- 2x_2} - x_3 \amp = 3
            </mrow>
          </md>
          with augmented matrix
          <m>\left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
            3 \amp -2 \amp -7 \amp -1 \\
            -1 \amp 3 \amp 6 \amp 1 \\
            2 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 3
          \end{array} \right]</m>.
          Carry out the operations of
          <xref ref="eg-elementary-operations" text="type-global" /> in matrix form.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Each display below starts from the original augmented matrix independently.</p>
        <p>
          <term>Interchange the first two equations (two rows),</term> <m>r_1 \leftrightarrow r_2</m>:
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              -1 \amp 3 \amp 6 \amp 1 \\
              3 \amp -2 \amp -7 \amp -1 \\
              2 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 3
            \end{array} \right].
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Multiply the first row by <m>-2</m>,</term> <m>-2r_1</m>:
          <md>
            \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
              -6 \amp 4 \amp 14 \amp 2 \\
              -1 \amp 3 \amp 6 \amp 1 \\
              2 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 3
            \end{array}\right].
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Add <m>3</m> times the second equation to the first equation,</term>
          <m>3r_2 + r_1</m>:
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              0 \amp 7 \amp 11 \amp 2 \\
              -1 \amp 3 \amp 6 \amp 1 \\
              2 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 3
            \end{array} \right].
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-echelon-forms">
    <title>Row-Echelon and Reduced Row-Echelon Matrices</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-row-echelon">
      <title>Row-Echelon Matrix</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The first nonzero entry in a nonzero row is its <term>leading entry</term>.
          A matrix is in <term>row-echelon form</term> (<term>REF</term>), or is a
          <term>row-echelon matrix</term>, if
          <ol>
            <li><p>All rows consisting entirely of zeros are at the bottom.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>The leading entry of each nonzero row is strictly to the right of the
              leading entry of the row above it.</p>
            </li>
            <li><p>All entries below each leading entry are zero.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="eg-row-echelon-shape">
      <title>The Shape of a Row-Echelon Matrix</title>
      <p>
        <md>
          \begin{bmatrix}
            0 \amp \square \amp * \amp * \amp * \amp * \amp * \amp * \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \square \amp * \amp * \amp * \amp * \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \square \amp * \amp * \amp * \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \square \amp * \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
          \end{bmatrix}
        </md>
        Here <m>\square</m> can be any nonzero real number, and <m>*</m> can be any real
        number.
      </p>
    </example>

    <definition xml:id="def-reduced-row-echelon">
      <title>Reduced Row-Echelon Matrix</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A matrix is in <term>reduced row-echelon form</term> (<term>RREF</term>), or is
          a <term>reduced row-echelon matrix</term>, if
          <ol>
            <li><p>It is in row-echelon form.</p></li>
            <li><p>The leading entry in each nonzero row is <m>1</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Each leading <m>1</m> is the only nonzero entry in its column.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="eg-reduced-row-echelon-shape">
      <title>The Shape of a Reduced Row-Echelon Matrix</title>
      <p>
        <md>
          \begin{bmatrix}
            0 \amp 1 \amp * \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp * \amp 0 \amp * \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp * \amp 0 \amp * \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp * \amp 0 \amp * \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp * \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
          \end{bmatrix}
        </md>
        Here <m>*</m> can be any real number.
      </p>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-pivots">
    <title>Pivots, Basic Variables, and Free Variables</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-pivot">
      <title>Pivots and Variables</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                A <term>pivot position</term> in a matrix <m>A</m> is a location in
                <m>A</m> that corresponds to a leading entry in a row-echelon form of
                <m>A</m>, that is, in a row-echelon matrix row equivalent to <m>A</m>. A
                <term>pivot column</term> is a column containing a pivot position.
                A leading entry need not equal <m>1</m> in row-echelon form;
                in reduced row-echelon form, every leading entry is <m>1</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                For an augmented matrix, the <term>basic variables</term> correspond to
                pivot columns of the coefficient matrix. The augmented column represents
                constants, not a variable, and is excluded when identifying basic or free variables.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>The variables corresponding to nonpivot columns of the coefficient matrix
              are called <term>free variables</term>.</p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      There is a question hiding in this definition. A matrix can be carried to
      row-echelon form by many different sequences of row operations, and different
      sequences can produce different row-echelon matrices (for instance, scaling a row of
      one gives another). Could two of them have their leading entries in different
      places, so that <q>pivot column</q> depends on who did the computation? The answer
      is no, because of the following theorem, which we state now and use freely.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-rref-unique">
      <title>Uniqueness of the Reduced Row-Echelon Form</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Every matrix is row equivalent to one and only one reduced row-echelon matrix.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p>
      The next section gives an algorithm that reaches <em>a</em> reduced row-echelon
      matrix; the theorem says it is <em>the</em> reduced row-echelon matrix of
      <m>A</m>, called the <term>reduced row-echelon form of <m>A</m></term>. Any
      row-echelon matrix row equivalent to <m>A</m> can be finished off (by scaling rows
      and clearing entries above leading entries) into this same reduced matrix without
      moving its leading entries, so every row-echelon form of <m>A</m> has its leading
      entries in the same positions. Pivot positions, pivot columns, and hence basic and
      free variables, are therefore properties of the matrix itself. We omit the proof.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="eg-identifying-pivots">
      <title>Identifying Pivots and Free Variables</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose that the following matrix is the augmented matrix of a system of linear
          equations:
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccccccc|c}
              1 \amp -3 \amp 4 \amp -2 \amp 5 \amp -7 \amp 0 \amp 4 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 8 \amp 0 \amp 3 \amp -7 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2
            \end{array} \right].
          </md>
          Identify the pivot columns, the basic variables, and the free variables.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Note that the matrix is already in row-echelon form (though not reduced: there
          are nonzero entries above some leading entries). The leading entries sit in
          positions <m>(1,1)</m>, <m>(2,3)</m>, <m>(3,4)</m>, and <m>(4,7)</m>, so the
          pivot columns are columns <m>1</m>, <m>3</m>, <m>4</m>, and <m>7</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Accordingly, <m>x_1</m>, <m>x_3</m>, <m>x_4</m>, and <m>x_7</m> are the basic
          variables, and the remaining variables (columns <m>2</m>, <m>5</m>, and
          <m>6</m>) <mdash /> that is, <m>x_2</m>, <m>x_5</m>, and <m>x_6</m> <mdash />
          are the free variables.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      Knowing what the target looks like, we can now describe how to get there: the next
      section gives the algorithm that transforms any matrix to row-echelon form, and then
      to reduced row-echelon form, by elementary row operations.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-ch1-matrices">
    <title>Practice: Reading and Writing Matrices</title>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-ch1-missing-coefficients">
      <statement><p>Form the augmented matrix for <m>x+3z=4</m>, <m>2y-z=5</m>,
      using variable order <m>x,y,z</m>. Then translate it back into equations.
      Why must the missing coefficients be recorded?</p></statement>
      <answer><p><m>\left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}1\amp0\amp3\amp4\\0\amp2\amp-1\amp5\end{array}\right]</m>.
      Reading the rows returns the stated equations. Zeros keep each variable in its
      designated column.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-ch1-echelon-pivots">
      <statement><p>Is
      <m>\left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}2\amp1\amp0\amp4\\0\amp0\amp-3\amp6\end{array}\right]</m>
      in row-echelon form? Is it reduced? Identify the basic and free variables.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>It is in row-echelon form, but not reduced because the leading entries
      are not <m>1</m>. The basic variables are <m>x_1,x_3</m>; <m>x_2</m> is free.
      The last column is the constant column and has no associated variable.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-ch1-classify-echelon">
      <statement><p>Classify each matrix as in reduced row-echelon form, in row-echelon
      form but not reduced, or not in row-echelon form. For those not in row-echelon
      form, say which condition of <xref ref="def-row-echelon" text="type-global"/> fails.
      <md>
        \text{(a)}\;\begin{bmatrix}1\amp2\amp0\\0\amp1\amp3\end{bmatrix},\quad
        \text{(b)}\;\begin{bmatrix}1\amp0\amp0\\0\amp0\amp1\end{bmatrix},\quad
        \text{(c)}\;\begin{bmatrix}0\amp1\\1\amp0\end{bmatrix},\quad
        \text{(d)}\;\begin{bmatrix}1\amp0\amp2\\0\amp0\amp0\\0\amp1\amp3\end{bmatrix},\quad
        \text{(e)}\;\begin{bmatrix}2\amp0\amp1\\0\amp0\amp3\end{bmatrix},\quad
        \text{(f)}\;\begin{bmatrix}1\amp4\amp0\amp5\\0\amp0\amp1\amp2\\0\amp0\amp0\amp0\end{bmatrix}.
      </md></p></statement>
      <answer><p>(a) Row-echelon but not reduced: the <m>2</m> sits above the leading
      <m>1</m> of row <m>2</m>. (b) Reduced row-echelon. (c) Not row-echelon: the leading
      entry of row <m>2</m> is to the left of the leading entry of row <m>1</m>.
      (d) Not row-echelon: a zero row is not at the bottom. (e) Row-echelon but not
      reduced: the leading entries are <m>2</m> and <m>3</m>, not <m>1</m>.
      (f) Reduced row-echelon.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</section>
